9 Browser Dashboard
*******************

The command ‘autoslip-roam-html-open’ writes the whole hierarchy to one
HTML page and opens it in the web browser.  The page is in the optional
file ‘autoslip-roam-html.el’, which also loads ‘autoslip-roam-nav.el’.

     (require 'autoslip-roam-html)
     (global-set-key (kbd "C-c n w") #'autoslip-roam-html-open)

9.1 Features of the Page
========================

   • Only the root notes show at the start.  Each root unfolds level by
     level.
   • The number beside a note is the count of all notes below it.
   • A filter box narrows the tree to the matching notes and unfolds the
     chains that lead to them.  Press ‘/’ to start and ‘Esc’ to clear.
   • Two buttons unfold or fold the whole tree.
   • The page remembers which notes you unfolded, so it looks the same
     after you write it again.
   • Notes without a parent note are listed in a section at the bottom.
   • The page is one file with no outside resources.  It works without a
     network connection, and it prints with all chains unfolded.

   The page is a snapshot.  Run the command again to bring it up to
date.  Roots and orphans follow the same rules as in the navigation
menu.

9.2 Links Back to Emacs
=======================

With the default value of ‘autoslip-roam-html-link-type’, each note is
an ‘org-protocol://roam-node’ link that opens the note in Emacs.  This
needs three things: ‘(require 'org-roam-protocol)’ in your init file, a
running Emacs server, and an operating system that sends org-protocol
links to ‘emacsclient’.  On macOS the last item needs a small helper
application.  When that is not set up, use one of the other values:

     (setq autoslip-roam-html-link-type 'file) ; link to the Org files
     (setq autoslip-roam-html-link-type nil)   ; a page without links


